# Development Services

`docker-compose.yml` contains the complete GoForj development-service catalog.
Every generated service, including MySQL and PostgreSQL, is dormant until its Compose profile is enabled.
Change only the owner-controlled `COMPOSE_PROFILES` value in `.env`; enabling or
disabling an already-rendered service does not require another project render.

```env
COMPOSE_PROFILES=nats,jaeger,adminer
```

Then start the selected services:

```bash
docker-compose up -d
```

Profile names are exact and comma-delimited. A token such as `redis-debug` does
not enable `redis`. Unknown tokens are preserved so an owner-managed
`docker-compose.override.yml` can use the same activation workflow.

## Disable All Local Dependencies

Project creation enables profiles for the selected local dependencies, including
`mysql` or `postgres` for the selected server database. To disable all generated
Compose services, set:

```env
COMPOSE_PROFILES=
```

No rerender is needed. `forj dev` skips generated Compose startup and
container-specific database readiness and creation work for disabled services.
Application database settings and auto-migrations still apply; configure an
external database if the App needs one. Process environment variables override
`.env`, so clear an exported `COMPOSE_PROFILES` value too if one is set.

To stop containers that are already running, use `forj down` or
`docker-compose --profile "*" down`. Clearing the profiles alone does not stop
containers or remove their volumes. Owner-defined unprofiled services and
custom startup tasks continue to follow the owner's Compose configuration.

