SFTPGo is intentionally not an automatic Storage provider. Its default admin
bootstraps the control plane, but a usable FTP or SFTP integration also needs a
protocol user, home permissions, and an explicit host-key decision. Those are
owner choices rather than deterministic project-generation defaults.

Toxiproxy publishes one proxy listener so projects can route a chosen local
dependency through it. GoForj does not guess the upstream service or rewrite
App endpoints when the profile starts.

## Component-Backed Profiles

Mailpit, VictoriaMetrics, and Grafana now use the same profile lifecycle as the
rest of the catalog while retaining their established component behavior.

| Profile | Default host endpoints | Activation and credentials |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `mailpit` | SMTP `127.0.0.1:1025` after applying the legacy-bind safety setting; UI `http://127.0.0.1:8025` | Seeded for the Mail component and local SMTP; no generated authentication |
| `victoriametrics` | `http://127.0.0.1:8428` after applying the legacy-bind safety setting | Seeded for Observability; the component adds its generated `vmagent` |
| `grafana` | `http://127.0.0.1:13001` after applying the legacy-bind safety setting | Seeded for the Grafana component; admin `admin` / `admin`; also starts VictoriaMetrics and uses the generated dashboards when the component is present |

New renders seed these profile tokens when their corresponding components or
local provider requirements are selected. During the first rerender of an
older project, GoForj detects exact unprofiled `mysql`, `postgres`, `mailpit`,
`victoriametrics`, and `grafana` services and appends the matching tokens without removing owner
profiles. This preserves their previous startup behavior while moving future
activation into `COMPOSE_PROFILES`. After this one-time migration, rerenders
preserve the owner's selection, including an empty value.

The dev summary and resource registry show Mailpit, VictoriaMetrics, and
Grafana links only while their exact profiles are active. Credentials are not
included in those links.

## Bindings, Credentials, And Data

Catalog ports use `DEV_SERVICE_IP_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1` and therefore bind to
loopback by default. The legacy `IP_ADDRESS` variable remains an explicit
override for Redis, Mailpit, VictoriaMetrics, Grafana, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.
To expose one of those services to other hosts, set:

```env
IP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0
```

Change `DEV_SERVICE_IP_ADDRESS` when another host must reach the other catalog
services. Replace all development credentials before exposing a service beyond
the local machine. Quote edited secrets with single quotes in
`.env` so spaces remain intact and characters such as `$` and `#` are not
interpreted as dotenv syntax:

```env
RUSTFS_SECRET_KEY='replace-with-a-local-secret'
OPENSEARCH_INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD='G0Forj-Choose-A-New-Local-2026!'
```

Stateful recipes use named volumes where restart persistence is useful.
Removing a token from `COMPOSE_PROFILES` changes the next Compose selection but
does not stop an already-created container or delete its data. After disabling
a profile, run `forj down` or the all-profile teardown before starting the new
selection:

```bash
docker-compose --profile "*" down
```

Ordinary teardown does not remove named volumes. Delete a service volume only
when a deliberate destructive local reset is appropriate.

## OpenSearch Host Requirements

OpenSearch needs at least 4 GB available to Docker. Linux and WSL hosts also
need `vm.max_map_count=262144`; follow the OpenSearch Docker installation guide
for the persistent host setting. The generated single-node stack keeps
OpenSearch security enabled and is intended only for local development.

The `admin` password bootstraps a fresh OpenSearch data volume; changing only
`OPENSEARCH_INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` after initialization does not rotate the
stored password and makes the generated health check use the wrong value.
Rotate an existing password with OpenSearch security tooling and update `.env`
at the same time. Removing only the OpenSearch named volume reinitializes it
and permanently deletes its local indexes.

## Project-Specific Profiles

Additional tools belong in owner-managed `docker-compose.override.yml`. Give
each service an exact `profiles` entry and add that token to
`COMPOSE_PROFILES`. GoForj preserves the override file and unknown profile
tokens, so project-specific services use the same no-rerender workflow as the
built-in catalog.
